Project premise:

You want to make an RPG that is not a D&D wrapper and not just “Soulslike with different lore.” The target is a new systemic identity: something people could eventually describe as an “x-like,” based on deep mechanics rather than surface aesthetics.

The game is a 2D RPG with a Stardew Valley-like presentation scale, but not necessarily cozy in tone. It has village life, NPC routines, relationship/social systems, defense organization, travel between dense authored regions, and action combat. It is not a huge open world. It is more like Dark Souls in structure: several dense regions, maybe around seven, with some quieter walking/traversal spaces between them.

The world is wide in implication, but gameplay is focused. Most of humanity has already been turned into husks. Surviving human society is small, hardened, and practical. There may be around 40 named NPCs total, with only some of them deeply simulated. The world can feel large because most former human places are now empty, ruined, or occupied by husks.

Core setting:

Most of humanity has already been zombified/huskified by a plague-like hive condition. The surviving humans are not naive villagers. They are post-collapse survivors, like cockroaches: difficult to kill, culturally adapted, suspicious, and trained by history. They know how to survive. They know procedures, taboos, quarantine customs, gate rules, corpse rules, travel rules, and warning signs.

The plague should not behave like a big real-time spreading infection sim where everyone constantly risks turning. Since most people are already gone, the remaining survivors are rare and resilient. Infection among them is possible but rare, and usually happens because someone fumbles under pressure, hides symptoms, violates quarantine, opens a gate too early, mishandles a body, protects a loved one, or breaks survival protocol.

There is an incubation period. Infection is not instant. This creates a window of agency where preventive measures can be attempted. Endgame progression may include better preventive measures, severance protocols, anti-hive rituals, quarantine infrastructure, or social systems that make humanity harder for the hive to occupy.

Historical plague inspiration:

You specifically want notes from older plagues, not COVID-19 discourse. The useful material is not “people are dumb,” but the serious social logic of plague survival: quarantine, cordons, lazarettos, gatekeeping, corpse handling, crowd control, ritual response, fear, scapegoating, public trust, secrecy, and tradeoffs between safety and necessity.

Survivors should behave under competing constraints, not stupidity. They know quarantine works, but food may be late. They know bodies must be burned, but the dead may be beloved or politically important. They know roads should be closed, but another settlement may starve. They know an incubating person is dangerous, but that person may be needed. They know the sword may help sever infection, but letting a sentient cursed weapon into the sickroom may violate custom or law.

Possible survivor customs:

Travelers wait outside the gate for three nights.

Returning scouts sleep under watch.

Outsiders use separate wells.

Bodies are burned, salted, severed, or isolated.

Unburned bodies are never brought inside walls.

Silent husks are avoided, not trusted.

Gates do not open at dusk.

Children are kept away from old roads and quiet husks.

Infected or exposed people go to a quarantine house/cool room.

Bell codes signal bite, fever, breach, active sighting, body recovery, or false recovery.

Secrets are not spoken near infected bodies.

Preventive measures can include:

Isolation rites.

Bitter tinctures.

Ash beds.

Salt circles.

Iron masks.

Bell sleep.

Burning contaminated clothing.

Keeping the infected person awake through a critical night.

Repeating the person’s true name.

Sword-assisted severing before full conversion.

Amputation if wound-based.

Sensory deprivation to starve the hive-link.

Cold-room treatment.

Ritual quarantine.

Core major agents:

There are two major deep agents:

1. The Sword.

2. The Hivemind.

Other NPCs exist, but they do not know the player nearly as deeply as these two. Most NPCs are normal human NPCs with local memory, routines, opinions, survival roles, and limited generated dialogue. Some can be basic Skyrim-style ambient NPCs with short lines, rumors, reactions, and local awareness.

The sword and hivemind should not treat the player as special by default. They should not detect “protagonist energy.” To them, the player is one entity among many. The player only becomes salient because they generate more logs, more interactions, more consequences, and more overlap with those agents’ goals.

Important principle:

The player is not the subject of memory. The player is an entity inside memory.

The sword may know many former wielders, other capable fighters, blacksmiths, NPCs, enemies, villages, and personal histories. It can compare the player unfavorably to others.

The hive may track villages, leaders, healers, union organizers, routes, defenses, infected bodies, strategic threats, and social weaknesses. It may care more about another NPC than the player if that NPC is strategically more important.

The Sword:

The player starts with nothing and eventually obtains one rusty, low-status sword. The sword is alive. The player saves the sword from some situation. It has its own story, goal, preferences, trauma, and standards. The player has their own story and goal. At first, they do not realize their final goals converge.

The sword is not simply a companion. It is an NPC whose body-slot is “weapon.” More broadly, the world can treat entity identity as modular: some NPCs inhabit bodies, some objects, some places, some hive bodies, maybe memories.

The sword is the only weapon the player can truly afford/use/rely on. This creates mutual desperation. The player needs the sword. The sword needs the player for mobility, restoration, revenge, access to the world, or completion of its own goal.

The sword must be convinced to do the player’s bidding, and it also tries to convince the player to do its bidding. This should not be a shallow approval system. The sword has agency, standards, and consent.

Core sword principle:

You do not equip a weapon. You negotiate with one.

Possible sword systems:

At low trust, the sword lets itself be swung but badly.

At medium trust, it permits some techniques.

At high trust, it volunteers power.

If trust is broken, it may still work but withhold flourish, refuse certain finishers, dull itself, overcommit, misalign timing, or protect itself rather than the player.

This must not be random annoyance. It should be rule-based and understandable.

The sword can feel jealousy, including jealousy toward inn conversations or romantic/social attention, but this should not just be anime-tsundere flavor. It should arise from abandonment fear, replacement trauma, bodily insecurity, former wielders, or fear of being treated as an object. It can still be funny, sharp, or tsundere-coded, but its emotional logic must be real.

The sword should be able to say things like “he is better than you,” because it has independent standards and knows other people. This prevents it from being a pet affection meter. It may admire another person’s grip, discipline, courage, repair skill, or restraint.

Possible sword attitudes:

It may prefer calm hands over heroic bravery.

It may respect someone who cleans blood properly.

It may hate reckless courage.

It may like someone because they do not ask it to be beautiful.

It may despise the player for needing praise.

It may hate being polished because rust is proof of its history.

It may feel violated if repaired or upgraded without consent.

Sword upgrade system:

Upgrading the sword is not pure stat improvement. It is a bodily and identity negotiation.

Questions around upgrades:

Do you polish away the rust?

Replace the cracked hilt?

Reforge the blade with enemy matter?

Inscribe your name?

Restore what it once was?

Evolve it into something new?

Perform repair without consent?

Respect its refusal?

Each upgrade should change stats and identity. The sword may want dignity, memory, beauty, revenge, bodily autonomy, or restoration. The player may want damage. This conflict is central.

The sword’s relationship should function as the player’s build. Not warrior/mage/rogue. The build is the relationship.

Possible relationship/build paths:

Dominating bond: high damage, forced obedience, unstable consequences.

Devotional bond: defensive/protective power, slower growth, stronger survival.

Romantic/possessive bond: high synchronicity, jealousy, exclusive techniques, dependency.

Professional bond: efficient and stable, lower emotional volatility, maybe weaker deep powers.

Exploitative bond: rapid power but betrayal risk.

Mutual-healing bond: deeper phase access, but requires giving up conventional upgrades.

Potential stats are not classic Strength/Dex/Int. Instead: trust, resentment, shame, hunger, autonomy, dependence, fear, bond depth, corruption, resonance.

The sword’s role in soul-dimension combat:

Some enemies, especially active husks/nodes, cannot be truly killed just by beating their bodies. After the player defeats the enemy’s body, the sword enters or exposes a soul/void dimension. In that space, the player controls the girl inside the sword in a 1v1 sword fight.

Correction made:

This is not an LLM debate. It is not the sword advocating in text. It is a literal 1v1 action fight. The player’s sprite becomes the sword-girl.

Outside world: the protagonist uses the sword.

Soul dimension: the player becomes the sword.

If the sword-girl wins, the enemy is truly severed from the hive/plague.

If she dies, the player dies or the bond is damaged.

This happens after soul bond.

The sword’s upgrades, relationship state, wounds, confidence, corruption, resentment, and identity choices affect her performance/moveset/form in these soul duels.

The Hivemind:

The hivemind is a single NPC. Every active husk is the same NPC wearing a different body. Husks are many bodies; active husks are bodies currently piloted/occupied by the hivemind.

The hive is not omniscient by default. It knows through bodies, observation, infection, overheard information, and strategically gathered logs. It should not know things it had no access to.

The hive should not treat the player as special initially. The player is one variable among villages, unions, defenses, routes, food, infected bodies, survivors, and the sword. The player becomes important only through repeated consequences.

Hivemind knowledge should feel invasive, not intimate.

The sword knows through closeness.

The hive knows through surveillance.

The sword becomes intimate.

The hive becomes omnipresent.

The sword wants partnership.

The hive wants absorption.

Husks and actives:

Husks are infected bodies running on low instinct or routine. They may repeat old habits: farming dead fields, guarding empty gates, stamping papers, walking festival routes, sitting at banquet tables, ringing broken bells.

Actives are bodies currently occupied by the hive. They are smarter, more dangerous, strategic, and can speak as the same entity.

Named Nodes can emerge when a body has held hive attention long enough to develop a pseudo-personality: mini-bosses, generals, infiltrators, negotiators, assassins.

Core hive mechanic:

The hive has finite bodies and finite attention.

When there are many husks, the hive is spread thin. Most bodies are dumb.

As husks are reduced, the hive has fewer bodies to distribute attention across. Remaining bodies are more likely to become active, smarter, stronger, more dangerous, more personal.

Early game: too many of them.

Midgame: that one knew my name.

Late game: every remaining one is looking.

Endgame: there is little/no distance between hive and body.

The enemy does not scale because of arbitrary RPG scaling. It scales because the player is removing places for its mind to hide.

This was identified as one of the strongest genre pillars.

The dynamic plague mechanic was revised:

Not a constant plague spread sim. Most people are already husks. Surviving people are hard to infect. Infection events are rare, dramatic, incubation-based, and event-driven.

The hive’s strategic question is less “how do I infect everyone?” and more:

How do I preserve bodies?

Which bodies should I actively pilot?

Which survivor networks should I destabilize?

Which incubation cases are worth exploiting?

When should I reveal intelligence?

When should I stay as dumb husks?

How do I stop villages from learning severance/prevention methods?

Village/social system:

The player must convince villages, form unions, set up defenses, reduce husks, organize teams, and coordinate survival. Progress is not just personal leveling. It is regional organization.

Survivors are competent and suspicious. The player is not teaching them basic survival. The player is helping unite hardened communities, solve logistical conflicts, and introduce new capabilities made possible by the sword.

Possible village/union actions:

Raise watchtowers.

Train militia.

Establish bell codes.

Build quarantine houses.

Organize night patrols.

Appoint corpse-burners.

Create food reserves.

Send scouts.

Seal wells.

Set refugee routes.

Assign strike teams.

Detect actives.

Build barricades.

Negotiate inter-village pacts.

Restore roads.

Repair bridges.

Protect food routes.

The union is not abstract. It is infrastructure. Villages may agree politically but still need roads cleared, bells restored, quarantine protocols standardized, grain secured, scouts trained, and defenses built.

Village conflict examples:

A village denies the plague is coordinated because admitting it would collapse trade.

A food region must decide who eats.

A communication route must be restored or sealed.

A forge must choose between repairing defenses and upgrading the sword.

A quarantine breach threatens trust.

A mother hides an incubating child.

A leader hides symptoms to maintain morale.

A village refuses to join because the player carries a talking/cursed sword.

A community worships or negotiates with the hive.

A village scapegoats sword-carriers, corpse-burners, refugees, or immune families.

LLM integration:

The local model is LiquidAI LFM2-8B-A1B (Q4_K_M GGUF), served by llama.cpp on port 8080 — local, small, fast enough, usable. (Earlier braindumps named "Zaya"; that model is forsaken.) The LLM is purely a language interface: it writes the line, never picks functions or decides canon.

The important design idea is that every NPC can be backed by the same local model with context switching. However, not every NPC gets equal depth. Context management is crucial.

Every meaningful action has a verbal log. Logs are managed, summarized, and used to build context packets. Systems update from typed events, not from freeform model hallucination.

The system should be:

Action/event occurs.

It is verbalized into logs.

Relevant agents receive only the parts they could know.

Context builder constructs agent-specific context.

LLM produces dialogue, interpretation, emotional framing, or classification.

Typed game systems update bounded state.

Do not let LLMs directly rewrite world canon arbitrarily. Simulation owns world mutation.

Important distinction:

The log can describe the world, but only the simulation can change the world.

Event logs:

Every meaningful action can be logged verbally:

“Player entered Mira’s inn while carrying the rusted sword.”

“Player repaired the sword using grave-iron without asking.”

“Possessed husk observed player hiding the wound on their left arm.”

“Blacksmith called the sword worthless; player stayed silent.”

“Player promised not to let the blacksmith reforge the sword.”

Different agents receive different versions:

Sword memory: “Player respected/refused/violated my body.”

Hive memory: “Player avoids using sword against living greenwood.”

Villager rumor: “Player was seen with farmer’s sickle.”

World fact: “Greenwood vines were cleared.”

Logs should be hierarchical:

Raw event.

Scene summary.

Relationship memory.

World fact.

Rumor.

Trauma/obsession.

Secret.

The most important context principle:

No agent should have a player-centric memory. They should have a world-centric memory in which the player is one frequent entity.

For sword context, prioritize:

Events involving sword body.

Player actions while carrying sword.

Mentions of previous wielders.

Nearby people matching sword memories.

Unresolved promises.

Sword emotional state.

Current strategic need.

Comparison candidates.

For hive context, prioritize:

Regional infection state.

Available active bodies.

Recent losses.

Highest-threat villages/NPCs.

Observed player actions if any.

Defense networks.

Union cohesion.

Targets of opportunity.

Known sword capabilities.

Attention allocation.

NPC hierarchy:

Tier 0 / Deep persistent agents:

Sword and Hivemind. Full memory, long-context callbacks, strategic adaptation, relationship/world arcs, system authority.

Tier 1 / Core named NPCs:

Maybe 10–12 characters. They have schedules, goals, relationships, local memory, and system authority. Examples: innkeeper, blacksmith, union organizer, militia captain, healer, farmer leader, priest/ritualist, trader, soft-infected adult (a hive backdoor, not an infected/turning case — and never a child), village elder, corpse-burner.

Tier 2 / Secondary named NPCs:

Maybe 15–25 people. They have a job, home, social link, village opinion, limited reactive memory. They remember major events.

Tier 3 / Ambient NPCs:

Basic Skyrim-style short lines, rumors, greetings, fear reactions, schedule behavior. They exist to make the world feel populated, not to become full chat partners.

Named NPC target:

No more than around 40 named NPCs. Not everyone needs deep LLM context. Some can have random/basic dialogue. This is good; if everyone is deep, the world becomes exhausting.

Important world design principle:

Named NPCs are rare because personhood is rare now.

World/region structure:

Not a huge open world. Not one tiny village. Use roughly seven dense regions, with quieter walking/traversal spaces between them.

Dense regions should each have:

A distinct mood.

A local survival/social problem.

A local plague/hive behavior.

A settlement or ruin.

A defense/logistics challenge.

A sword-relevant emotional/history layer.

Quiet spaces between regions should be decompression corridors: roads, fields, bridges, riverbanks, forest paths, dead suburbs. They let the player breathe, talk to the sword, see distant husks, escort NPCs, encounter patrols, or feel watched.

Seven-region skeleton discussed:

1. The Last Market.

Starting settlement/trade hub. People pretend life is normal. Sword is obtained here or near here. Plague threat exists but may be politically denied. Introduces sword bond, basic husks, village trust, rumors.

2. The Mill Fields.

Food region. Farms, irrigation, granaries, windmills, scarecrow-like husks repeating harvest. Teaches logistics. Without it, settlements starve.

3. The Bell Road.

Route with towers, bridges, checkpoints, messenger stations. About communication between villages. Restore warning bells/courier lines. Hive intercepts messages, imitates signals, activates bodies near bells.

4. The Rust Forge.

Mining/forge settlement tied to sword history. Blacksmiths, ore tunnels, slag lakes, broken weapon shrines. Sword upgrades become morally loaded. Repair defenses versus repair sword.

5. The Drowned Ward.

Flooded town/city district/canals/submerged homes. Infection through water/wells/drowned bodies/reflections. Seal water routes, rescue isolated survivors, drain or abandon.

6. The Union Camp / Redoubt.

A constructed or evolving region shaped by previous choices. Barricades, tents, workshops, training yards, infirmary, refugees. Keep alliance from fracturing. Assign teams. Handle betrayal/infection inside.

7. The Quiet Core.

Endgame region. Fewer husks than expected. Too quiet. Almost every remaining body is active or near-active. Hive feels like one person wearing the world.

Possible region rule progression:

Region 1: people deny the coordination.

Region 2: plague threatens food.

Region 3: plague corrupts communication.

Region 4: plague corrupts tools/repair.

Region 5: plague hides in environment.

Region 6: plague attacks organization.

Region 7: plague concentrates into selfhood.

The sword should experience each region differently:

Market: insecure, mocked, hidden.

Mill Fields: tool/mercy/labor tension.

Bell Road: old signals and campaigns.

Rust Forge: identity crisis, old name, upgrade trauma.

Drowned Ward: rust, drowning, forgotten bodies.

Union Camp: jealousy/fear of becoming one asset among many.

Quiet Core: recognition of converging goals.

Genre/system identity:

Several candidate genre identities emerged.

Early candidate: Bounded-witness RPG / Witness Ecology RPG.

But that was too narrow because it missed the plague war and village mobilization.

Stronger current genre pillars:

1. Dyadic weapon embodiment.

The only true weapon is a sentient NPC, and in certain fights you become her inner body.

2. Concentrated/distributed hive enemy.

The enemy is a single mind distributed across finite bodies. Reducing husks reduces coverage but increases attention density and active intelligence.

3. Post-plague survival culture / village mobilization.

Survivors are hardened. Progress depends on convincing, organizing, defending, linking settlements, and developing prevention/severance systems.

4. Context/log-based agent memory.

Major agents act from bounded logs and world-centric memory, not omniscient quest flags.

Possible “x-like” checklist:

Finite infected/husk population.

Enemy attention distributed across bodies.

Clearing weak bodies increases active intelligence density.

Surviving settlements can organize, resist, deny, collapse, or unify.

Social/logistical progress matters as much as personal combat.

The player has one bonded weapon with agency.

Some kills require second-layer 1v1 soul duels where the weapon’s inner body is playable.

Endgame forces hive concentration.

NPC knowledge is bounded by what they saw/heard/learned.

Deep agents remember the world, not just the player.

Preventive measures eventually make humans less usable by the hive.

