Important thematic axis:

Possession versus partnership.

The hive possesses bodies.

The sword does not want to be possessed.

The player must not treat the sword as a mere tool.

Upgrades risk possessing/rewriting the sword’s identity.

Romance can become possessiveness.

The hive wants unity through erasure.

The sword/player relationship can become unity without erasure.

Combat asks who controls the blade.

Soul duels are battles for severance and selfhood.

The final goal:

The player and sword have different apparent goals but the same final goal, revealed over time. They agree on destination but may disagree on method. The sword’s ancient wound and the player’s personal quest converge at the same source.

Endgame:

The final hive is not beaten merely by killing all husks. It can be beaten because humanity becomes harder to occupy: through union, protocols, severance, quarantine discipline, bell networks, defensive infrastructure, and the sword’s ability to sever active agents.

A strong ending condition:

The hive loses because the remaining humans stop becoming available bodies.

Important vibe lines / principles:

You do not equip a weapon. You negotiate with one.

The sword is not your companion. The sword is your build.

Outside world: the sword is your weapon. Soul dimension: you are the sword.

The sword knows you through closeness. The hive knows you through surveillance.

The player is not special by ontology. The player is special by log density.

The world does not track you. The world remembers you through bodies that were present.

Nothing knows everything. Everything remembers something.

Named NPCs are rare because personhood is rare now.

