The sword can glow when it detects enemies nearby, but only after rust is removed. This is a functional maintenance upgrade, not a memory/emotional tradeoff.

Rust should not be tied to memory. Keep it simple: rust blocks the sword’s sensory channel. Removing rust restores detection.

Rust/detection upgrade chain:

Rusted blade: no glow; maybe vague danger sense only.

Edge cleaned: better cutting, maybe very weak flicker.

Fuller cleaned: enemy proximity glow unlocked.

Guard contact cleaned: readable hilt vibration/audio pulse, useful even sheathed.

Polished detection line: distinguishes threat intensity by glow rhythm.

This chain requires grindstone, forge tools, fine polishing kit/oil/wire brush, and training with the sword to interpret glow patterns.

The glow detects active hive bodies, attended enemies, root nodes, and later environmental hive organs/mud/listening stones. It should not detect normal humans, animals, pure traps, or dormant bodies unless upgraded.

In Arghanzza, glow helps detect hidden pine-husks, hive-mud, active root nodes, and environmental hive organs. Silent rocks may absorb/dim the glow. The hive may later mimic or spoof glow under high pressure, especially in Arghanzza.

Rust removal is an early upgrade lock: maintenance unlocks perception.

This supports Arghanzza gating: with detection upgrade, crossing is easier; without it, the player must solve environmental clues manually.

